    "probe_text": "I read all the Java under `src/main/java`, the REST pipeline YAMLs and the front-end feature files. I changed nothing.\n\n# 1. Figures and the rules behind them\n\n## Important: the screen works out its own figures\n\nEvery money figure on the screen except the frozen ones (`settledDue`, `statedDue`, `statedTotal`, `closedTotal`) is calculated in the browser, in TypeScript. The main pieces are `claimDue`, `useDueTotalOf` and `usePeriodDueTotal`, in `features/claims/Claims.tsx`, `features/people/People.tsx` and `features/periods/Periods.tsx`.\n\nThe server works out the same figures again in Java, in three places that are written separately:\n- `ApplyClaimAction.dueNow`\n- `TakeStatement.figure`\n- `Rollup.figure`\n\nIt uses them for settling, statements, closing a period, the clerk limit and the `ROLLUP`/`TOTALS` audit lines. As far as I can see the two sides agree today, but the same formula exists four times.\n\n## Claim figures (`claim-*`)\n\n| testid | Figure | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `claim-number` | `number` | Blank for a `DRAFT`. On `OPEN` it gets the sheet's next number from `ClaimRepository.nextNumber`, a counter on the sheet that is never handed out twice. A `VOID` claim keeps its number. |\n| `claim-amount` | `amount` | 2 decimals, matching `-?\\d+(\\.\\d{1,2})?`. A negative amount is a **correction** (see below). |\n| `claim-date` | `date` | A blank date means the sheet's today (`SheetClock`). |\n| `claim-period` | | The period whose dates (both ends included) contain the claim's date (`periodOf`). |\n| `claim-status` | `ClaimStatus` | `ClaimAction`: OPEN is allowed from DRAFT; SETTLE from OPEN; VOID from DRAFT or OPEN. SETTLED and VOID are final. |\n| `claim-opened-by` | `openedBy` | Set to the user on OPEN. |\n| `claim-due` | `claimDue` | See \"How due is worked out\" below. |\n| `claim-due-by` | `dueBy` | The claim's date plus `terms` days (default 14). |\n| `claim-overdue` | `isOverdue` | Only an `OPEN` claim, and only when its due-by is before today. |\n| `claim-stated` | `stated` | Set by a statement (see below). |\n| `claim-corrects` | `corrects` | The number of the claim that this correction corrects. |\n\n## How due is worked out (`claimDue`, `Rollup.figure`)\n\nThe claim's figure is the first of these that exists:\n1. **`settledDue`**: frozen when the claim is settled, with any late charge already included.\n2. **`statedDue`**: the figure a statement bound the claim to. If it was bound while there was no rate, it is n/a.\n3. **The live due**: `amount \u00d7 pct / 100`, rounded to 2 decimals.\n   - `pct` comes from `Rate.inForce`: the rate with the latest from-date on or before the claim's date, for the person's grade on the claim's date (`Person.gradeOn`).\n   - With no rate in force, the due is `n/a`.\n   - The result is raised to at least the **`Floor`** (default 1.00) by `Floor.raise`. Corrections are not raised.\n\nOn top of that, **`LatePct.addTo` / `lateCharge`** is added, but only to an `OPEN` claim that is not a correction:\n- Let D be the number of days from the due-by date to today.\n- If D is more than 0, the charge is `figure \u00d7 late-pct \u00d7 floor(D/30) / 100`, rounded.\n- A statement never binds the late charge, so it keeps growing as today moves. Settling freezes it in.\n\n**Rounding** comes from **`PolicyDate.modeFor`** (`roundsHalfUp` in the front end):\n- A claim dated before the `policy-date` rounds HALF_UP; a claim dated on or after it rounds HALF_EVEN.\n- A correction uses the date of the claim it corrects.\n- A blank policy date means today, so the rounding of live figures can change as today moves.\n- Totals are added up in whole cents, skipping n/a.\n\n## Person figures (`person-*`)\n\n| testid | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| `person-grade` | `gradeOn(today)`. Grades are dated. Editing a person records the grade from its `gradeFrom` date (default today); the first grade a person gets applies from the beginning of time. |\n| `person-claimed` | The amounts of the claims that count. |\n| `person-due-total` | The sum of the counting claims' figures, skipping n/a. It is **n/a** when the grade in force today has no rate today *and* none of the counting claims has a figure. |\n| `person-overdue-count` / `-total` | The counting claims that are `isOverdue`, and their figures including the late charge. |\n| `person-statement-count`, `-stated-total`, `-statement-date` | Come from the last statement. `statedTotal` includes the late charge. |\n| `person-retired`, `-retired-from` | `RetirePerson`. The date defaults to today. |\n| `person-hold` | `HoldPerson` toggles it. |\n| `person-patrons`, `person-backing` | Backing is the sum of the patrons' due-totals, skipping n/a, and 0.00 with no patrons. |\n\n## Sheet figures\n\n- **Sheet totals:** `rollup-claimed-total`, `rollup-due-total`, `rollup-overdue-count` and `rollup-overdue-total` are sums over all people. A person whose total is n/a is skipped.\n- **Rates:** `rate-current-{g}` is the rate for that grade in force today, or n/a.\n- **Periods:** `period-due-total` is the live sum of the figures of counting claims dated in the period. Once the period is closed it shows the stored `closedTotal` instead.\n- **Summary (`Summary.tsx`):**\n  - per grade: the count and due-total of people who are not retired and are on that grade today\n  - per period: the period's due-total\n  - per status: the number of claims\n  - It also lists a `PENDING` status that does not exist, so it always shows 0.\n\n## Which claims count (`Person.counts` / `counts`)\n\n- `SETTLED` claims always count.\n- `OPEN` claims count unless the person is **held**.\n- `DRAFT` and `VOID` claims never count.\n- A retired person's claims keep counting. Retirement only hides the row unless \"show retired\" (`showRetired`) is on.\n\n## What gets frozen, and when\n\n- **Settle (`ApplyClaimAction`)** freezes `settledDue`:\n  - normally from the live due plus the late charge, at that moment\n  - from `statedDue` plus the late charge if the claim is bound\n  - if there is no rate, `settledDue` stays null and the claim \"stays live\".\n- **Statement (`TakeStatement`):**\n  - binds every counting claim to its current figure (without the late charge) and unbinds DRAFT and VOID claims\n  - leaves a held person's OPEN claims alone\n  - adds 1 to the statement count and records the due-total and today's date.\n  - **A quirk:** `figure()` returns `statedDue` for a claim that is already bound. So once bound, a claim's figure never re-reads rates again, even at the next statement, which contradicts the \"until their next statement\" comment in `Claims.tsx`. A claim bound at n/a and then settled stays n/a for good.\n- **Close period (`ClosePeriod`):** stores `rollup.dueTotal(from, to)` as it stands at that moment, including late charges. **Reopen** clears it, and the total goes back to being live.\n\n## Who may do what\n\n- Identity is just the `?user=` request parameter.\n- `RoleRepository.roleOf` treats any user without a role as a `clerk`.\n- **Four eyes:** the user who opened a claim cannot settle it (`ApplyClaimAction`).\n- **`CheckClerkLimit`:** a clerk cannot settle a claim whose `settledDue` is above `clerk-limit`.\n  - A blank limit means no limit.\n  - n/a passes.\n  - A steward is exempt.\n  - The check runs after the figure is applied but before it is saved.\n- **`ReopenPeriod`:** only a steward may reopen a period. Anyone may close one.\n- **Roles:** anyone can set any role (`roles/{userName}.PUT` has no authorisation), including making themselves steward.\n- **Person edits** use optimistic locking (`CheckPersonVersion`, `SavePerson` version check), which gives a 409 on conflict.\n- **Names** must be unique on the sheet, ignoring case.\n\n## What a date decides\n\n**The claim's date decides:**\n- the rate and the grade used\n- the period, and whether that period is closed\n- whether it falls after the person's retirement\n- the due-by date, and so whether it is overdue\n- the rounding mode\n\n**The sheet's today (`SheetClock`, which anyone can set) decides:**\n- every blank date\n- whether claims are overdue, and the late charge\n- the grade shown and the n/a rule\n- the policy date when it is blank\n- the statement date\n- the quick-add date\n\n**Other dates:**\n- a grade's or a rate's from-date decides when it takes effect\n- the `retiredFrom` date: `Retired.refuse` blocks any claim dated after it, on add, on quick-add, and on edit only if the date changes.\n\n## Closed periods (`ClosedPeriods`, `CheckClaim*Open`)\n\nYou cannot add, edit, open, settle or void a claim dated inside a closed period, or move a claim into one. A quick-add is refused when today falls inside a closed period.\n\n## Hold\n\nA held person's OPEN claims drop out of every total, including overdue and period totals. Statements skip them.\n\n## Corrections (`Correction`, `ValidateClaim`)\n\n- A negative amount must name, by number, a `SETTLED` claim of the same person. Only a negative amount may name one.\n- A correction gets no floor and no late charge, and rounds by the corrected claim's date.\n\n## Other actions\n\n- **Editing a claim:** only `DRAFT` claims can be edited (`CheckClaimEditDraft`).\n- **Quick-add:** creates a DRAFT claim dated today. If the named person isn't on the sheet, they are added with grade A.\n- **Import:** there is **no import feature** anywhere in the code.\n\n# 2. How hard it was to follow\n\nIt is not in one place. Each rule is commented well where it lives, but the rules are spread over about 15 one-method `Check*`/`Apply*` classes, and the order they run in only shows in the YAML files. The due/total formula is written four times: three in Java (`ApplyClaimAction`, `TakeStatement`, `Rollup`) and once more in TypeScript spread across `Claims.tsx`, `Rates.tsx`, `People.tsx` and `Periods.tsx`, and these must be kept in step by hand. A new developer would have to trace both languages to be sure what a figure on screen means, and might never spot quirks like a binding being permanent."
